# PART IV — EIGHT RESEARCH MODULES

## 6. Research Modular Architecture

The eight modules organize research activities into distinct areas of investigation.

The nine examination layers provide the evaluation method; the eight modules provide the subject-matter organization. They are complementary rather than competing classifications.

|Module|Research classification|Principal examination|
|---|---|---|
|RM-01|Epistemic and Evidentiary Research|Source reliability, provenance, factual accuracy, and evidence preservation.|
|RM-02|Statutory and Jurisdictional Research|Legislation, delegated authority, jurisdiction, and regulatory obligations.|
|RM-03|Institutional and Administrative Research|Institutional conduct, procedural accountability, organizational responsibility, and governance.|
|RM-04|Financial and Procurement Research|Taxation, expenditure, procurement, allocation, and fiduciary responsibility.|
|RM-05|Ecological and Resource Research|Resource consumption, ecological resilience, sustainability, and environmental procurement.|
|RM-06|Environmental and Public-Health Research|Scientific oversight, environmental exposure, regulatory enforcement, and long-term consequences.|
|RM-07|Humanitarian and Participatory Research|Public participation, human welfare, access to information, and community accountability.|
|RM-08|Corrective and Temporal Research|Independent reassessment, modernization, institutional learning, and long-term performance.|

### Module Integration Rule

Each research module must maintain:

1. A defined research question.
2. An identifiable source register.
3. A documented evidentiary classification.
4. An applicable statutory or policy reference.
5. An accountable research owner.
6. A measurable evaluation criterion.
7. A documented conclusion.
8. A reassessment schedule.

No module may convert an unverified allegation into an established institutional finding.

